==Icelandic==
{{wikipedia|hungur|lang=is}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|is|/ˈsvauŋkʏr/}}
* {{rhymes|is|auŋkʏr|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|is|non|svangr||slender, slim, thin}}, from {{inh|is|gem-pro|*swangaz||thin, slim}}. The original meaning in Germanic was probably 'curved inwards', discernible from the derived Old Norse {{m|non|svangi||flank, groin}}, ie 'the sides of the abdomen which curve inwards slightly'. Within Icelandic the meaning developed from 'thin' to 'feeling hungy'<ref>{{R:gem:EWGP|head=swanga-|page=572}}</ref>. Cognates are {{cog|fo|svangur||hungry}}, {{cog|da|svang||thin, famished}}, {{cog|no|svang||hungry}}, dialectal {{cog|sv|svånger||thin, hungry, empty}}, Middle {{cog|sv|svanger||slender, emaciated, very thin}}, {{cog|gmh|swanger||flexible, slender}}<ref name="blondal">{{R:Íslensk orðsifjabók 1989}}</ref><ref>{{R:non:AnEW|head=svangr|page=564}}</ref>.

Further origin unsure, perhaps related to Old Irish {{m|sga|seng||narrow, thin}}. Not related to Danish {{m|da|svanger}}, Norwegian {{m|no|svanger}}, and German {{m|de|schwanger||pregnant}}.

====Adjective====
{{is-adj|svengri|comp2=svangari|svengstur|sup2=svangastur}}

# [[hungry]], [[peckish]]
#: {{ux|is|Ertu '''svöng'''?|Are you '''hungry'''?}}
#: {{ux|is|Ég er mjög '''svangur'''.|I'm really '''hungry'''.}}
# {{lb|is|obsolete}} [[thin]], [[slender]], [[slim]]

